+
+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+The record store must decide how independently edited copies of the same visit (identified by a client-generated UUID) become a single current record. Updates can arrive days after their starting state was fetched, and arrival order carries no evidence of authority. Two workers commonly correct the same visit after comparing field notes.
+
+The policy determines three things other nodes depend on: the submission metadata tablets must retain, the shape of coordinator review work, and which visits can enter the partner's Friday CSV. It is hard to reverse once tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it, because both inherit the submission envelope and the unresolved-work representation from this node.
+
+The scientific fields — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes — contain edits that are sometimes independent and sometimes coupled. Revising a species identification and revising its cover estimate are one scientific correction expressed in two fields; correcting an observer's name and adding a missing species are unrelated. The server cannot infer which fields are coupled from their names.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Preservation of every submitted edit** (major; turns on the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*). A losing or superseded submission must remain retrievable and attributed.
+- **Inspectability of the exported value** (major; turns on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). A coordinator and the science partner must be able to see how the current value arose.
+- **Coordinator operability** (major; turns on *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). Review must stay manageable for two part-time coordinators.
+- **Review workload** (major). How many visits require coordinator attention before export.
+- **Maintenance burden** (major). The rules and code the team must sustain.
+- **Scientific coherence of automatically combined records** (major; turns on the non-goal *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*). An automatically produced current record must not silently join edits whose scientific relationship the server cannot see.
+- **Offline continuation** (moderate; turns on *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*). The policy must not require connectivity to submit or to keep working.
+- **Submission size / sync cost** (minor). Bytes per submission over flaky tablet connections.
+
+Latent criteria that cut against the emerging recommendation were probed and kept: submission size and offline continuation both favor the merge option (smaller deltas, more visits auto-approved). They are recorded above.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every edit is a complete visit state submitted with the revision ID it started from. A revision whose base equals the visit's current revision fast-forwards; a revision whose base does not is retained as a competing revision and the visit is marked unresolved. A coordinator resolves by selecting one candidate as current. Original revisions are immutable and retained. No automatic combination of fields ever occurs.
+
+**Demonstration.** A tablet submits `{visit_id, base_revision_id: R3, payload: {...all fields...}}`. The server accepts it as R4 when R3 is current, or files a review item with candidates {R3-current, R4} when another revision superseded R3.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Preservation of every submitted edit: **strong** — each submission is stored whole and attributed.
+- Inspectability: **strong** — the current value is exactly one submitted revision, traceable to its author and base.
+- Coordinator operability: **partial** — the review primitive is simple (compare complete visits), but every conflict, including unrelated-field edits, enters the queue.
+- Review workload: **weak** — competing complete visits are always review work, inflating the queue.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong** — no field rules to write or keep correct.
+- Scientific coherence: **strong** — the server never combines fields; a coordinator does the judging.
+- Offline continuation: **strong** — no connectivity needed to submit or continue.
+- Submission size: **weak** — full visit payload per edit.
+
+**Summary.** Buys simplicity, preservation, and coherence by spending coordinator review time and submission size.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit supplies its base state and a structured set of field changes. Disjoint changes combine automatically; overlapping changes route to review. The server maintains rules per field kind — scalar fields, observer lists, species entries — to decide what counts as disjoint. A submission is smaller than a full visit, and many edits that would conflict under whole-visit revisions resolve without a coordinator.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two edits from base B, one changing `notes` and one changing an observer's name, combine automatically. Two edits that both change the same cover estimate overlap and require review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Preservation of every submitted edit: **strong** — originals are retained; the merged current record is an additional representation.
+- Inspectability: **partial** — the current value is a computed combination, so provenance must be reconstructed from field-level metadata.
+- Coordinator operability: **partial** — fewer items reach review, but the review view must expose field-level provenance.
+- Review workload: **strong** — disjoint edits merge without a coordinator.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak** — rules for scalars, lists, and species entries must be written and kept correct.
+- Scientific coherence: **weak** — independence is inferred from field names, so the merge can silently combine edits that are scientifically coupled but differently named, such as a species identification and its cover estimate.
+- Offline continuation: **strong**.
+- Submission size: **strong** — deltas are smaller than full visits.
+
+**Summary.** Cuts review volume and submission size, at the cost of maintained merge rules and a class of silent mis-combinations those rules cannot see.
+
+### Arrival-order last-write-wins
+
+**Description.** The most recently received edit replaces the prior state, with no review. Recorded to make the boundary explicit.
+
+**Analysis.** **Eliminated** on preservation of every submitted edit: it discards an edit on arrival order alone, which the store may not treat as authority (tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*).
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on scientific coherence and maintenance burden (both major), with review workload accepted as the cost. Field-aware merge is strongest exactly where whole-visit revisions are weakest — review workload and submission size — but weak on scientific coherence: by keying independence off field names, it can combine a species-identification edit with a cover-estimate edit without noticing they are coupled, producing a plausible but scientifically wrong current record that no coordinator sees. The team judges a larger, simpler review queue the smaller risk than merge rules that must never miss a coupling they cannot see. Preservation, inspectability, and coordinator operability all favor whole-visit revisions as well; offline continuation is neutral between the two.
+
+The choice turns on the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and on *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*; it accepts a cost against *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*, mitigated because the review primitive is simple even though the queue is larger.
+
+**Implications — what this forecloses, and what it obligates.**
+
+- **Forecloses** automatic combination. Every conflict costs a coordinator action; review volume is reduced only by managing how often workers edit the same visit simultaneously, not by the server.
+- **Obligates tablet-sync** to: retain a base revision ID per locally-held draft, and update it after a successful submission; represent a competing/unresolved submission to the worker; carry a full visit payload per submission; and decide how further edits to an unresolved visit are handled.
+- **Obligates review-export** to: provide a revision comparison screen showing complete candidate revisions side by side rather than field-level diffs or provenance; hold unresolved visits out of the CSV; and expose a resolution operation that records the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs.
+- Both siblings **inherit** the submission envelope and the unresolved-work representation from record-store. These are cascade obligations noted here, not co-ownership of this decision.
